        "<strong>D’varim (<em>דְּבָרִים</em>) — “words.”</strong> The Hebrew name of this book is its second word — <em>eleh ha-d’varim</em>, “these are the words.” The English title <em>Deuteronomy</em> follows the LXX <em>Deuteronomion</em>, “second law,” a translation of <em>mishneh ha-torah ha-zot</em> in 17:18 (which actually means “a copy of this law”). The Greek translators’ slight overreach gave the book its Western name. <em>D’varim</em> captures something essential: this is the book of Moses’ words — three long sermons delivered on the plains of Moab in the final weeks of his life.",
