        "",
        "love Yahweh your God with all your heart",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The V’ahavta.</strong> Following directly on the Sh’ma, this verse forms the second paragraph of the Sh’ma prayer in Jewish liturgy. It commands what cannot be commanded — love. The traditional resolution: <em>v’ahavta</em> here means not the feeling but its embodied expression — the actions, study, and discipline that constitute loving God in practice. Jesus pairs this with Lev 19:18 as the heart of the Law (Mark 12:29–31, Matt 22:37–40).",
