    (
        8,
        3,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;man doth not live by bread only, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of the Lord doth man live&#x27; (Deu 8:3), Ephrem reads the manna-pedagogy as the canonical anchor cited by Christ at the first Temptation (Mt 4:4 / Lk 4:4 — verbatim citation). The manna teaches that humanity&#x27;s deepest hunger is for the Word, not for bread; the Eucharist resolves both hungers in one. Tewahedo eucharistic theology cites Deu 8:3 + Jn 6:51 (&#x27;I am the living bread which came down from heaven&#x27;) + Mt 4:4 as the canonical triple-witness: Christ is the Word-who-is-Bread, fulfilling the Deuteronomic pedagogy in the Eucharistic anaphora.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 8:1-10. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Mt 4:4, Jn 6:51.",
    ),
