    (
        10,
        16,
        "",
        "Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Mulot orlat l’vavkhem (<em>מַלְתֶּם</em> <em>עָרְלַת</em> <em>לְבַבְכֶם</em>).</strong> The metaphor of heart-circumcision appears here for the first time and is developed in Jer 4:4 and Rom 2:29. Outward observance without inner transformation is the persistent prophetic critique. Deut 30:6 promises that God himself will perform this circumcision in the eschatological future — making it not just a command but a divine work, anticipating the new-covenant theology of Jeremiah and Paul.",
        "User original",
    ),
