        15,
        11,
        "",
        "the poor will never cease out of the land",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The realism of poverty.</strong> Verse 11 sits in tension with v. 4 (“there shall be no poor among you”) — the same chapter holds out both the ideal and the realistic acknowledgment. The resolution: poverty is not inevitable in principle (if the Sabbatical-year debt release is observed), but inevitable in practice (because it won’t be). Jesus alludes to this verse in Mark 14:7 — not to dismiss the poor but to invoke the whole context: the perpetual obligation that follows from their perpetual presence.",
        "User original",
