    (
        17,
        18,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On the king&#x27;s Torah — &#x27;when he sitteth upon the throne of his kingdom, he shall write him a copy of this law in a book&#x27; (Deu 17:18-19) — Ephrem reads the king-as-Torah-scribe command as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of the doctrine that the king is FIRST a guardian of the law, only secondarily a ruler of the people. The king does not stand above the Torah; he copies it with his own hand and reads it all the days of his life. Tewahedo Solomonic-dynasty self-understanding, codified in the Kǝbrä Nägäśt (Glory of the Kings), cites Deu 17:18 as the canonical Old-Testament warrant for the Ethiopian-emperor-as-Torah-guardian — the emperor at coronation receives both crown and Tabot, and is sworn to defend both.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 17:14-20. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Tewahedo dynastic witness: Kǝbrä Nägäśt.",
    ),
    (
        17,
        18,
        "c",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>THRONE</strong> (Hebrews kiss’e), a royal chair or seat of dignity (Deuteronomy 17:18; 2 Samuel 7:13; Psalm 45:6); an elevated seat with a canopy and hangings, which cover it. It denotes the seat of the high priest in 1 Samuel 1:9; 4:13, and of a provincial governor in Nehemiah 3:7 and Psalm 122:5. The throne of Solomon is described at length in 1 Kings 10:18-20. <em>[Reviewer: condense Easton's entry as needed.]</em>",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
