    (
        21,
        23,
        "",
        "he who is hanged is accursed of God",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>“Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.”</strong> The verse Paul cites in Galatians 3:13 to interpret the cross. The original referent is post-mortem display of an executed criminal — leaving the body exposed beyond sundown is forbidden because such a person is under God’s curse and would defile the land. Paul’s daring move in Galatians is to read Jesus’ crucifixion through this verse — Christ becoming the curse to redeem from the curse. The Targum Pseudo-Jonathan and the Temple Scroll (11QTemple) both extended the verse to crucifixion specifically, providing the interpretive bridge Paul’s argument relies on.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum, Pseudo-Jonathan",
    ),
