        15,
        "",
        "kill and eat meat within all your gates",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Profane slaughter.</strong> The verse marks one of Deuteronomy’s most consequential legal innovations: the desacralization of ordinary meat-eating. Earlier Pentateuchal law (Lev 17:3–4) treated all animal slaughter as cultic — every killing of an animal had to be brought to the sanctuary as a peace-offering, with the blood disposed of properly. Deuteronomy, anticipating cult centralization at one distant sanctuary, makes ordinary slaughter for food permitted "in all your gates" — within any town — provided the blood is poured out (12:16). The legal change is enormous: meat consumption is partially uncoupled from sacrifice, and a distinction between sacred and ordinary slaughter enters Israelite practice for the first time. Most scholars treat this provision as one of the strongest internal indicators that Deuteronomy was composed (or radically edited) during the seventh-century push toward Jerusalem-centric worship, when the practical impossibility of bringing every animal to one sanctuary required a legal accommodation. The verse quietly transforms how Israelites actually ate.',
        "User original",
