        "a people for his own possession",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>Am s’gullah (<em>עַם</em> <em>סְגֻלָּה</em>).</strong> "A treasured people" — the term <em>s’gullah</em> means a personal treasure, the kind a king might keep separately from the public treasury. The same term describes Israel in Exod 19:5, Deut 7:6, 26:18, and Mal 3:17 (where the righteous are Yahweh’s <em>s’gullah</em> "in the day that I make"). The Christian appropriation of the term — 1 Pet 2:9, "a people for his own possession" — applies the formula to the Church, with conscious dependence on the Septuagint Greek (<em>laos eis peripoiēsin</em>). The verse encodes Israel’s self-understanding as the deity’s personal portion among the nations — a status that is theologically positive (covenant election) and practically demanding (the dietary and ritual distinctiveness the chapter goes on to detail). The chosen-people theology has had a long and complicated history; the OT itself uses the term for both blessing and obligation simultaneously.',
